{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"9f5146ada53681e7","name":"card_unit_halifax","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Britain/WinterWar/units/card_unit_halifax","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"HALIFAX B Mk I","text":"Bombers you deploy (including this) deal their attack in damage to a random enemy.","flavorText":"The Handley Page Halifax was a four-engine heavy bomber used by the RAF and the Commonwealth air forces during WWII. The Halifax entered service in 1940 and made its operational debut in March 1941, participating in a daylight bombing raid on German captured port of Le Havre in France. Later that year, due to increased fighter opposition and a high casualty rate, the Halifax was withdrawn from daylight bombing operations and became part of the Pathfinder Force, marking routes and targets for the Main Force bombers. It played a core role in the bombing offensive against Germany, and at its peak, Bomber Command operated 76 Halifax-equipped squadrons. The early-built Halifax models faced performance issues, primarily due to the underpowered Merlin engines. However, with the introduction of the Halifax Mk III, powered by Bristol Hercules radial engines, it started to demonstrate superior performance against German fighter defenses. Following the invasion of Europe in 1944, the Halifax resumed daylight bombing operations, conducting strikes against enemy targets such as troop concentrations, gun emplacements, and the Atlantic Wall defenses. The Halifax was also used by Coastal Command for anti-submarine warfare, reconnaissance, and mine deployment. It served in various support roles, including glider tug, electronic warfare and special operations. The Halifax flew its last major operation in April 1945, and after the war it remained in service with RAF Coastal Command and Transport Command, Royal Egyptian Air Force, and the French Armée de l'Air until early 1952.","faction":"Britain","type":"bomber","rarity":"Rare","cost":8,"operationCost":3,"attack":5,"defense":4},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_halifax","title":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_halifax_title","SourceString":"HALIFAX B Mk I","LocalizedString":"HALIFAX B Mk I"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::bomber","attack":5,"defense":4,"range":2,"faction":"EFactionEnum::Britain","kredits":8,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_halifax_text","SourceString":"Bombers you deploy (including this) deal their attack in damage to a random enemy.","LocalizedString":"Bombers you deploy (including this) deal their attack in damage to a random enemy."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_britain","Key":"card_unit_halifax_history","SourceString":"The Handley Page Halifax was a four-engine heavy bomber used by the RAF and the Commonwealth air forces during WWII. The Halifax entered service in 1940 and made its operational debut in March 1941, participating in a daylight bombing raid on German captured port of Le Havre in France. Later that year, due to increased fighter opposition and a high casualty rate, the Halifax was withdrawn from daylight bombing operations and became part of the Pathfinder Force, marking routes and targets for the Main Force bombers. It played a core role in the bombing offensive against Germany, and at its peak, Bomber Command operated 76 Halifax-equipped squadrons. The early-built Halifax models faced performance issues, primarily due to the underpowered Merlin engines. However, with the introduction of the Halifax Mk III, powered by Bristol Hercules radial engines, it started to demonstrate superior performance against German fighter defenses. Following the invasion of Europe in 1944, the Halifax resumed daylight bombing operations, conducting strikes against enemy targets such as troop concentrations, gun emplacements, and the Atlantic Wall defenses. The Halifax was also used by Coastal Command for anti-submarine warfare, reconnaissance, and mine deployment. It served in various support roles, including glider tug, electronic warfare and special operations. The Halifax flew its last major operation in April 1945, and after the war it remained in service with RAF Coastal Command and Transport Command, Royal Egyptian Air Force, and the French Armée de l'Air until early 1952.","LocalizedString":"The Handley Page Halifax was a four-engine heavy bomber used by the RAF and the Commonwealth air forces during WWII. The Halifax entered service in 1940 and made its operational debut in March 1941, participating in a daylight bombing raid on German captured port of Le Havre in France. Later that year, due to increased fighter opposition and a high casualty rate, the Halifax was withdrawn from daylight bombing operations and became part of the Pathfinder Force, marking routes and targets for the Main Force bombers. It played a core role in the bombing offensive against Germany, and at its peak, Bomber Command operated 76 Halifax-equipped squadrons. The early-built Halifax models faced performance issues, primarily due to the underpowered Merlin engines. However, with the introduction of the Halifax Mk III, powered by Bristol Hercules radial engines, it started to demonstrate superior performance against German fighter defenses. Following the invasion of Europe in 1944, the Halifax resumed daylight bombing operations, conducting strikes against enemy targets such as troop concentrations, gun emplacements, and the Atlantic Wall defenses. The Halifax was also used by Coastal Command for anti-submarine warfare, reconnaissance, and mine deployment. It served in various support roles, including glider tug, electronic warfare and special operations. The Halifax flew its last major operation in April 1945, and after the war it remained in service with RAF Coastal Command and Transport Command, Royal Egyptian Air Force, and the French Armée de l'Air until early 1952."},"operationCost":3,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::WinterWar","isInPermanentPool":true,"Effec